    "result_text": "Done. The change is implemented and the test suite is green.\n\n## What I did\n\nI updated `AssignCustomerCode` (`src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/function/owner/AssignCustomerCode.java`) \u2014 the step in the `POST /api/owners` pipeline that assigns an owner's `customerCode` after the owner is built and before it's saved.\n\nPreviously it assigned the raw `'<REGION>-<HASH8>'` code with no collision handling. Now it:\n\n1. Computes the base code `'<REGION>-<HASH8>'` as before.\n2. Collects the `customerCode`s of all already-persisted owners via `ownerRepository.findAll()` (the new owner isn't saved yet, so it's naturally excluded).\n3. If the base code collides, appends `-<n>` starting at `n = 2` and increments until it finds a code not already in use, then assigns that de-duplicated code.\n\nThe loop guarantees the smallest `n \u2265 2` because it tries `base` first, then `base-2`, `base-3`, \u2026 in order, stopping at the first free value. I injected `OwnerRepository` as a parameter (resolved by type, following the same idiom as `CountNamesakes` and `EnsureUniqueOwnerIdentity`) and updated the Javadoc to document the de-duplication rule.\n\n## Verification\n\n`./mvnw test` \u2192 **BUILD SUCCESS**, `Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0` (the `AcceptanceTest.coreCustomerCodesStayUnique` guarantee-based test, which is the only test in the suite).\n\nNote: as the test's own comment explains, a genuine hash collision can't be forced black-box (it requires an identical normalized-telephone + last-name, which the pipeline's telephone-uniqueness check already blocks), so the suite asserts the invariant that distinct owners always get distinct codes rather than exercising the `-<n>` suffix path directly. The de-dup logic itself covers that path when a collision does occur.",
